<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Science | dw.com</title><subtitle type="html">Science</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss_en_science</id><updated>2026-10-13T16:08:19Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss_en_science" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-12526?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss_en_science" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Pope lung nodule: Could it be cancer?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e08abb0395814c05f7def40b747eddb2b0889fb9d71f289d05e212c1d226906e</id><updated>2026-10-13T16:08:19Z</updated><published>2026-10-12T14:41:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/pope-lung-nodule-could-it-be-cancer/a-79641503--9cf198017761.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/pope-lung-nodule-could-it-be-cancer/a-79641503?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The case of Pope Leo shows how small lung nodules are often found during routine health checks. Quick treatment is generally advised before nodules can grow to become larger life-threatening masses.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry><entry><title>Early-onset cancer rising worldwide: Causes, risk, prevention</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5bfe89df75249f262834a2a35014832915a24cb523b973005119db045e7167ea</id><updated>2026-10-13T16:08:19Z</updated><published>2026-10-08T13:26:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/early-onset-cancer-rising-worldwide-causes-risk-prevention/a-79597097--5416293e8076.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/early-onset-cancer-rising-worldwide-causes-risk-prevention/a-79597097?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Across Latin America, early-onset cancer rates have risen, especially of bowel, breast and cervical cancers. But it is a worldwide trend.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry><entry><title>Nobel Prize in chemistry goes to Henri B. Kagan and Kenso Soai</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f785327bee53988554fee8c9d69d73b8c77351969e8e51c7f33254291c9568ca</id><updated>2026-10-13T16:08:19Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T09:51:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/nobel-prize-in-chemistry-goes-to-henri-b-kagan-and-kenso-soai/a-79573924--1eccf7a6b469.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/nobel-prize-in-chemistry-goes-to-henri-b-kagan-and-kenso-soai/a-79573924?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Henri B. Kagan and Kenso Soai won the Nobel Prize in chemistry for their groundbreaking discoveries related to the origin of chirality and life's building blocks.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Suspected pneumonic plague in Siberia: What we know about the disease</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f3540b721877012b250ad2005cecb4aadbce8d119253b152f58c17805bf7667c</id><updated>2026-10-13T16:08:19Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T09:44:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/suspected-pneumonic-plague-in-siberia-what-we-know-about-the-disease/a-79547919--6c7e74a1d6ab.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/suspected-pneumonic-plague-in-siberia-what-we-know-about-the-disease/a-79547919?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">After a worker at a Russian plague research institute in Siberia died October 2, 2026, nearly 200 of her contacts were under medical observation. But authorities there denied pneumonic plague had caused the death.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Nobelitis — How winning a Nobel Prize can give you delusions of grandeur</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:6d7f07718e792bfb1bc571dc822b1177a58dccc3230fa80ad163b34a4c188c60</id><updated>2026-10-13T16:08:19Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T08:58:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/nobelitis-how-winning-a-nobel-prize-can-give-you-delusions-of-grandeur/a-79525772--1ee420badedc.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/nobelitis-how-winning-a-nobel-prize-can-give-you-delusions-of-grandeur/a-79525772?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">After being chosen for one of the world's most prestigious awards, some Nobel laureates lose touch with reality and suddenly start spouting unscientific nonsense. They're afflicted by what some have called "Nobelitis."</summary><category term="Science" /></entry></feed>
